part B: error equals x^(n+1)/(1-x) exactly at every degree, and each error is x times the one before
part C: x = 2, f(x) = -1; the partial sums P_n(2) are 2^(n+1) - 1, printed below
part C: n =  0  P_n(2) =      1  error =     -2
part C: n =  1  P_n(2) =      3  error =     -4
part C: n =  2  P_n(2) =      7  error =     -8
part C: n =  3  P_n(2) =     15  error =    -16
part C: n =  4  P_n(2) =     31  error =    -32
part C: n =  5  P_n(2) =     63  error =    -64
part C: n =  6  P_n(2) =    127  error =   -128
part C: n =  7  P_n(2) =    255  error =   -256
part C: n =  8  P_n(2) =    511  error =   -512
part C: n =  9  P_n(2) =   1023  error =  -1024
part C: n = 10  P_n(2) =   2047  error =  -2048
part C: the error at n = 10 grows in absolute value, so the degree does not help at x = 2
part D: first derivative of g at 0 from the definition: (g(h) - g(0)) / h
part D: h = 1e-1, quotient = 1.0e-01
part D: h = 1e-2, quotient = 1.0e-02
part D: h = 1e-3, quotient = 1.0e-03
part D: h = 1e-4, quotient = 1.0e-04
part D: h = 1e-5, quotient = 1.0e-05
part D: h = 1e-6, quotient = 1.0e-06
part D: at the six steps printed the quotient equals |h|; Example 4.1 gives the limit 0, so g'(0) = 0
part D: one-sided quotients of g'(x) = 2|x| at 0: right (g'(h) - g'(0)) / h, left (g'(-h) - g'(0)) / (-h)
part D: h = 1e-1, right quotient =  2, left quotient = -2
part D: h = 1e-2, right quotient =  2, left quotient = -2
part D: h = 1e-3, right quotient =  2, left quotient = -2
part D: h = 1e-4, right quotient =  2, left quotient = -2
part D: h = 1e-5, right quotient =  2, left quotient = -2
part D: h = 1e-6, right quotient =  2, left quotient = -2
part D: at the six steps printed, the right quotient is +2 and the left quotient is -2 (Proposition 4.2)
